1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,200
Same history. One-eighth the width.

2
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:03,800
Seven times less memory.

3
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:06,500
Why doesn't the past need to be as wide as the present?

4
00:00:06,900 --> 00:00:12,250
This time we zoom into one detail of ProxyFormer: asymmetric embeddings.

5
00:00:12,250 --> 00:00:16,600
It starts saving memory before the first layer even runs.

6
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:20,700
First: where does the memory actually go?

7
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:25,517
The embedding layer turns each token into a vector of d numbers.

8
00:00:26,517 --> 00:00:30,283
A sequence of L tokens becomes an L-by-d matrix.

9
00:00:30,283 --> 00:00:36,417
Every layer keeps activations like this, and training holds on to them for backprop.

10
00:00:36,417 --> 00:00:41,033
So memory scales roughly with length times width times depth.

11
00:00:42,033 --> 00:00:47,117
With long context, L is huge. You can shrink L, or you can shrink d.

12
00:00:47,117 --> 00:00:51,333
Proxy tokens shrink L. This video is about shrinking d.

13
00:00:52,133 --> 00:00:58,400
The key observation: history tokens and current tokens have completely different jobs.

14
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:04,867
Current tokens are few, but they predict the next word directly, so they need full width.

15
00:01:04,867 --> 00:01:11,800
History tokens are countless, but their only job is to be remembered and condensed into proxies.

16
00:01:11,800 --> 00:01:17,083
So give them different widths: 64 for the history, 512 for the present.

17
00:01:18,083 --> 00:01:24,883
In the code it's simply two embedding tables: d_history for the past, d_model for the present.

18
00:01:25,683 --> 00:01:30,567
The key: the history's local stream stays 64-wide in every layer.

19
00:01:31,767 --> 00:01:37,317
Each chunk of history is compressed into a proxy, and proxies are 512 wide.

20
00:01:37,317 --> 00:01:44,567
The generation side is 512 wide and attends only to the proxies, never to the narrow stream directly.

21
00:01:45,567 --> 00:01:51,633
At ratio 64, one proxy summarizes 64 tokens times 64 dims, 4,096 numbers, into 512.

22
00:01:51,633 --> 00:01:57,900
The stream is narrow, the proxy is wide: information is regrouped into wider channels.

23
00:01:57,900 --> 00:02:04,233
And the narrow stream persists, so detail one layer missed is still there for the next.

24
00:02:05,033 --> 00:02:11,300
Let's do the math: a 716,800-token history, 16-bit, one hidden-state tensor per layer.

25
00:02:11,300 --> 00:02:16,183
At full width: 716,800 times 512 times 2 bytes, 734 MB per layer.

26
00:02:16,183 --> 00:02:19,750
The 64-dim history stream needs just 91.8 MB.

27
00:02:19,750 --> 00:02:24,700
There are only 11,200 proxies; even at 512 wide they take 11.5 MB.

28
00:02:24,700 --> 00:02:28,717
Together: 103 MB, 7.1 times smaller than full width.

29
00:02:28,717 --> 00:02:37,167
Over 10 layers, 7.3 GB versus 1.0 GB. Training keeps several such tensors per layer, so the savings stack the same way.

30
00:02:37,967 --> 00:02:44,567
The cost is one extra history table: 50K vocabulary times 64, about 3.2 million parameters.

31
00:02:44,567 --> 00:02:51,700
It's a fixed cost that never grows with the history, while the activation savings grow with length.

32
00:02:52,500 --> 00:02:59,633
Every measured result in the paper uses this setup: 512 on the generation side, 64 for the history.

33
00:02:59,633 --> 00:03:04,517
With ~21K tokens of history, memory falls from 15.6 GB to 2.9 GB.

34
00:03:04,517 --> 00:03:08,467
One 16 GB card trains on 716,800 tokens of history.

35
00:03:08,467 --> 00:03:15,133
A 64-dim history doesn't forget: multi-needle retrieval stays above 90% at a million tokens.

36
00:03:15,133 --> 00:03:18,100
And language-modeling quality holds.

37
00:03:18,100 --> 00:03:24,767
To be precise: these are results of the full design, not an ablation of the embedding alone.

38
00:03:25,567 --> 00:03:28,800
d_history is really a knob you can turn.

39
00:03:28,800 --> 00:03:34,467
Narrower saves more, wider keeps more detail; the paper's experiments use 64.

40
00:03:34,467 --> 00:03:40,467
A natural extension: make older history narrower still. That remains to be tested.

41
00:03:41,267 --> 00:03:45,683
Long history, narrow history. That's asymmetric embedding.

42
00:03:45,683 --> 00:03:54,133
The Proxy Token method is patent pending, and collaborations are welcome. Paper and code are here. Thanks for watching.
